-- Opening a module re-declares the opaque constants (postulates, abstract definitions) that it
-- re-exports when the module was loaded from its cache, so that copies multiply along the import
-- graph and are stored in the caches of modules rechecked later.
--
-- `open` skips a field already bound to the same value (already_bound in src/lang.ml), but
-- V.same_head (src/value.ml) only recognises folded definitions (Def, compared by id) and falls
-- back to physical equality, which fails for opaque constants re-evaluated from a cache.
--
-- Reproduce, from this directory (d is an ordinary definition, p a postulate, a abstract):
--
--   rm -f *.batto
--   batt --debug Main.batt > /dev/null                 # all modules checked and cached
--   batt --debug Main.batt | grep -cE '^DECL +p = '    # 2: C and D loaded from cache
--   echo >> B.batt; echo >> C.batt
--   batt --debug Main.batt > /dev/null                 # B, C, D rechecked against cached modules
--   batt --debug Main.batt | grep -cE '^DECL +p = '    # 3, and growing; for d it stays 1
--
-- In graytt, PropTrunc's postulates and the abstract lemmas of Equivalence were re-declared
-- dozens of times per module (51 copies of trunc when opening Fibration in GrayTT.batt), which made
-- Coslice-fibration, Hom-family, Yoneda and GrayTT.batt take minutes.
--
-- Expected: one declaration of p and a, like d. A possible fix: compare opaque constants in
-- V.same_head,
--   | Opaque (o, []), Opaque (o', []) -> o = o'
-- but this may not suffice: an ordinary alias of a postulate (p' = BaseAx.p, with BaseAx imported
-- qualified) is duplicated as well, while d = ⊤, e = ⊤ → ⊤ and f x = x are not.
--
-- Workaround: delete the caches (make clean) and check the entry file once: modules checked in
-- the same session are deduplicated correctly (GrayTT.batt: 35 s from scratch, then 2 s).

open import C
open import D
